  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Time Reversal",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a continuous-time signal from $x(t)$ to $x(-t)$ by mapping every time location $t_0$ to $-t_0$ and recognizing reversal as negative time scaling in the time-domain operation sequence.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Reversal",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Reversal",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Time reversal is the operation that mirrors a continuous-time signal across $t=0$. In this first pass, the only thing that changes is the time argument, so the signal values stay attached to their original heights.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the reversed signal as $y(t)=x(-t)$. The minus sign acts on time, not on amplitude, so this means \"read the original signal backward in time\" rather than \"flip the signal values.\"",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(-t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the base case, a point at $t_0=3$ moves to $-3$, and a point at $t_0=-2$ moves to $2$. The value attached to each point stays the same, so the reversal is a left-right swap in time, not a change in height.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "You can also think of time reversal as time scaling by $-1$. That viewpoint will matter later, but for now the key idea is simple: every time location $t_0$ maps to $-t_0$ while the signal value itself is preserved.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Reversal",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating Values of $x(-t)$ from $x(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A signal is given by the table $x(-3)=4$, $x(-1)=1$, $x(0)=0$, and $x(2)=5$. For $y(t)=x(-t)$, find $y(3)$, $y(1)$, and $y(-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a lookup in $y(t)=x(-t)$. To answer it, flip the query time first and then read the matching value from the original $x(t)$ table.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(3)=x(-3),\\; y(1)=x(-1),\\; y(-2)=x(2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now read those values from the table: $x(-3)=4$, $x(-1)=1$, and $x(2)=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $y(3)=4$, $y(1)=1$, and $y(-2)=5$. The time argument changes sign, but the signal value itself does not.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Reversal",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Mapping Time Locations Under Time Reversal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given labeled time locations at $t=-4$, $t=-1$, $t=0$, and $t=5$ on $x(t)$, trace the locations of the same features in $x(-t)$ and list the mirrored times in order.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a set of labeled times on $x(t)$. Under time reversal, each time coordinate flips sign, so the feature values stay attached while the locations move to the opposite side of $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-4 \\mapsto 4,\\; -1 \\mapsto 1,\\; 0 \\mapsto 0,\\; 5 \\mapsto -5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the mirrored locations are $4$, $1$, $0$, and $-5$. The value attached to each feature does not change; only the time label changes sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Ordered from left to right, the reversed signal has features at $-5$, $0$, $1$, and $4$. The point at $0$ stays fixed because $-0=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Reversal",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Time Reversal as Scaling by $-1$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A student sees $y(t)=x(-t)$ and thinks it is a shift. Use a labeled point such as $t_0=6$ to show the point moves to $-6$ and explain why the operation is time scaling by $-1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the minus sign inside the time argument. That means the transformation is of the form $y(t)=x(at)$ with $a=-1$, so time is being scaled, not shifted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(at),\\; a=-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the tracked point $t_0=6$. Under the scaling rule, $6$ maps to $-6$, so the same feature appears at the mirrored time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "6 \\mapsto -6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A shift would add the same offset to every time label, but here the sign of each time label changes instead. That is why the whole signal mirrors about $t=0$ and $0$ stays fixed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $y(t)=x(-t)$ is time scaling by $-1$, not a shift. The signal values stay attached to the same features while the time locations reverse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
